## Post-conditions

What must hold after it runs:

1. Every tensor returned matches the dumped output feature map within tolerance —
   not just the first. A module returning a tuple is checked element by element.
2. No non-finite values. The harness NaN-poisons this module's parameters before
   applying the recorded ones, so a weight the implementation never reads shows up
   as a NaN rather than passing on whatever was in memory.
3. The output is positionally aligned with the input: same batch and sequence
   layout, so the next module's recorded arguments still apply.

Tolerance is the harness's to set, not this module's: a single-module replay is one
step and held to a near-exact elementwise bar, while a boundary seen during
end-to-end emulation accumulates rounding from every layer before it and is gated
on cosine.

## Running it

```bash
python inference.py --module {{ module_ids[0] }}       # run it: error + latency
python verify.py --all-modules --all-samples          # gate every module and sample
```

`inference.py` launches `source.py` on the dumped weights and input, times it, and
compares the result against the dumped output. It prints the metrics in autohelix's
capturable form and exits non-zero when the output no longer matches, so it works
as the benchmark command of an optimization loop over this directory:

```
##autohelix[latency_ms=...]    median of the timed forward passes
##autohelix[cosine=...]        worst cosine against the dumped reference
##autohelix[max_abs_err=...]   worst absolute error
##autohelix[max_rel_err=...]   worst relative error
##autohelix[passed=1]          1 only when every tensor is within tolerance
```

`--repeat`/`--warmup` set how many passes are timed, `--device cuda` runs it on the
accelerator, and `--rtol`/`--atol` loosen the bar when you mean to trade accuracy.

`verify.py` exits 0 when every tensor matches, 1 when one does not, 2 when the
artifacts are unusable. That makes it the acceptance test for optimizing this module:
rewrite a kernel in `source.py`, or swap `inference.py` for another backend's
implementation, point `--run` at these same artifacts, and it tells you whether the
result still reproduces the reference.

## Files

| file | owner | what it is |
|---|---|---|
| `source.py` | yours | **the implementation** — this module's classes, copied verbatim; edit this to optimize it |
| `inference.py` | yours | the launcher: imports `source.py`, builds the class from the recorded config, loads the dumped weights |
| `verify.py` | the harness | decides whether the implementation is correct; regenerated every run |
| `config.json` | the harness | the config this module's subtree was built from, as recorded |
| `meta.yaml` | the harness | module ids, layers, shapes, byte counts |
| `README.md` | the harness | this file |

`source.py` and `inference.py` are written once and then left alone, so an edit here
survives the next run of the loop.
